5 true] [17592186235343 1062 #uuid "ba14b890-647d-5652-bc7d-4e60bf914736" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235343 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234962 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235344 1062 #uuid "ba7ea822-7e81-5b92-868c-db61c8f76070" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235344 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234955 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235345 1062 #uuid "cbccb7ec-3ac7-530b-b532-804bc12806c4" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235345 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234978 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235346 1062 #uuid "db552029-deeb-5855-8823-20b47a6c618d" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235346 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234974 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235347 1062 #uuid "ee49f421-8714-557f-8d08-faa83990dab1" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235347 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234979 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235348 1062 #uuid "f91de9bf-93b7-579a-8081-e9301748694c" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235348 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234956 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235184 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235349 1069 "q-120254" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235349 1071 "![](images/68/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235349 1072 #uuid "df097f49-5d4b-56bd-b0e8-a7fcc926dc0e" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235349 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235160 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235350 1069 "q-120257" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235350 1071 "![](images/55/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235350 1072 #uuid "16c001cc-439d-56cc-834f-f6d6a747b8e6" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235350 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/40/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235247 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235351 1069 "q-120260" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235351 1071 "![](images/73/73e0ac67304b5e7682abddb59fcd01a9e8aca88831a303bf5b31a72ff772228d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235351 1072 #uuid "67eef7d0-d993-5f1c-ad25-dae49fa99a98" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235351 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/24/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-3b=-3a-3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235102 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235352 1069 "q-128186" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235352 1071 "Find the function $L(x),$ the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $L(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235352 1072 #uuid "56cf6bdc-a3b8-584f-b317-ba1056c265ac" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235352 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=x{y}^{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=1\\cdot {2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+4(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235126 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235353 1069 "q-128187" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235353 1071 "Use the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235353 1072 #uuid "df7cd8fe-37e2-547f-a82b-4acd638be632" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235353 1074 "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235064 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235084 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235101 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1069 "q-128188" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1071 "We wish to improve the approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1072 #uuid "e503619d-21af-5f4b-b6eb-d5c17fa54595" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235354 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $c=1.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235022 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235355 1069 "q-128189" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235355 1071 "Use Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235355 1072 #uuid "17ab4fd5-a02b-55ea-bcd4-23bfc484ef65" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235355 1074 "We'll start with the table from part 3.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now proceed to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using Euler's method.\n\n**Step 1.** We compute the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{45}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{45}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{45}{16} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+\\frac{45}{16}=\\frac{93}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{45}{4}$ | $\\frac{45}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{93}{16}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{93}{16}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235094 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235356 1069 "q-128190" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235356 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235356 1072 #uuid "582624f5-a799-5fc6-8044-e08124e9009a" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235356 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & =\\frac{2-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{y(x)-2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[y(x)-2]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{y}^{′}(x)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(1)}{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (y(2))^{2}}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235158 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235357 1069 "q-128191" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235357 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}.$ Assume that $y\\ne 0$ and $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y=-2($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+K)^{-1}$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235357 1072 #uuid "778dd5ee-c1ff-5286-be7b-93fe7ada6aaf" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235357 1074 "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2C}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+2C}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+2C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+K} \\\\ y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235196 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235358 1069 "q-128192" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235358 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y(x)=\\,\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235358 1072 #uuid "93ff4d9b-f05d-54cb-bfec-91c45b5200dc" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235358 1074 "From part 6, we found the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{({x}^{2}+K)}^{-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -2{({1}^{2}+K)}^{-1} & =2 \\\\ 1+K & =-1 \\\\ K & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2({x}^{2}-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235136 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235359 1069 "q-128235" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235359 1071 "Find the derivative of $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235359 1072 #uuid "ecceb386-fdf1-5dd9-81d5-114b41a2975b" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235359 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=x-2$ and $v(x)={x}^{2}+4x+k,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)({x}^{2}+4x+k)-(x-2)(2x+4)}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4x+k)-(2{x}^{2}-8)}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+k+8}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235099 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235360 1069 "q-128236" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235360 1071 "Find the value of $k$ such that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is equal to $0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235360 1072 #uuid "ffc5990e-58cd-5fb4-8d5d-6325acc47d62" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235360 1074 "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+k+8}{{({x}^{2}+4x+k)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{-{0}^{2}+4\\cdot 0+k+8}{{({0}^{2}+4\\cdot 0+k)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve when $x=0$ equals $\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, using the fact that  the slope must be equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+8}{{k}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-8.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235131 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235190 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1069 "q-128237" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1071 "When $k=3,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1072 #uuid "530e92de-0b7f-501a-9a55-9f1f7a58c438" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235361 1074 "When $k=3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}=\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x+3}+\\frac{B}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above expression through by $(x+3)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\nx-2=A(x+1)+B(x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3$ gets rid of the $B$ term and allows us to solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-2 & =A(-3+1)+B(-3+3) \\\\ -5 & =-2A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1$ gets rid of the $A$ term and allows us to solve for $B:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-2 & =A(-1+1)+B(-1+3) \\\\ -3 & =2B \\\\ B & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235228 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235362 1069 "q-128238" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235362 1071 "Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235362 1072 #uuid "6f9f3c49-fab7-5715-8827-fab0d52bd33d" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235362 1074 "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3{|}^{5}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1{|}^{3})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|]}_{-5}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{4}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{4}^{3}}{{2}^{5}\\cdot {3}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235210 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235262 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1069 "q-128239" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1071 "When $k=4,$ the function $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants. What are the values of $A$ and $B?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,B\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1072 #uuid "aecc9a69-d844-5ba7-a1b4-ea127ca3d901" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235363 1074 "When $k=4,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+4x+4}=\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =A(x+2)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to solve for $A$ and $B,$ and both of these constants appear in the equation simultaneously. However, we can eliminate $A$ if we substitute $x=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2-2 & =A(-2+2)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve for $A.$ To do this, let’s start by substituting our value $B=-4$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =A(x+2)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A.$\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-2,$ since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x.$ So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-2 & =A(0+2)-4 \\\\ -2 & =2A-4 \\\\ 2 & =2A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235283 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235364 1069 "q-128240" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235364 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in the case when $k=4.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235364 1072 #uuid "6fcd05bc-de04-5bd0-81db-56dab7283570" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235364 1074 "Earlier, we found that when $k=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x-2}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\frac{4}{x+2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+1]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2] \\\\  & =[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186234986 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235365 1069 "q-128282" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235365 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235365 1072 #uuid "e3d72624-846c-59cd-8e4f-8dff9efadea7" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235365 1074 "We differentiate the function, applying the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+24\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{(3-1)}-3\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}+24\\cdot 1-7\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235058 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235366 1069 "q-128283" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235366 1071 "If $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235366 1072 #uuid "664f315d-ffcf-5ecf-bb73-9cba307bd19e" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235366 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-6x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ \n\n* Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x={x}_{1}=-4$ and $x={x}_{2}=2.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-4\\cdot 2=-8.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235139 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235140 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1069 "q-128284" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1071 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1072 #uuid "11d48983-8d2c-5031-ab51-9f45fb7ab1a7" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235367 1074 "The function is increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ \n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}-6x+24>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n-3(x+4)(x-2)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=-3(x+4)(x-2)$ has roots at $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/41/41fbc51820e147872fddfd0f124f0235a26513992f07a4b5681f2d8cb25d4a3b.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is increasing for $x\\in (-4,2).$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235004 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235051 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1069 "q-128285" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1071 "For which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1072 #uuid "624fba80-874f-5836-8e6f-22428b76964b" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235368 1074 "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24=-3(x+4)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the two critical points $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-5,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =-3(-5+4)(-5-2)=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0+4)(0-2)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-3(3+4)(3-2)=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $-4$ | $(-4,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n* At $x=-4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-4$ is a local minimum.\n\n* At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235191 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235272 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1069 "q-128286" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1071 "What are the global maximum and global minimum values of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,3]?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global maximum is ${f}_{max}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global minimum is ${f}_{min}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1072 #uuid "857b43bc-6a11-50db-94f8-30e8204a2443" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235369 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We already know the critical points of the function:\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-4$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, the critical points on the interval $[-1,3]$ are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2}+24(-1)-7=-33 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+24(2)-7=21 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}-3(3)^{2}+24(3)-7=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step 2.\n\n* The greatest value is $f(2)=21,$ so the global maximum is ${f}_{max}=21.$\n\n* The smallest value is $f(-1)=-33,$ so the global minimum is ${f}_{min}=-33.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235098 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235370 1069 "q-128287" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235370 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{″}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{″}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235370 1072 #uuid "278a7bce-1b90-5d01-b44e-3fac1eca5038" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235370 1074 "We already know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2}-6x+24)}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x-6\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235017 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235276 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1069 "q-128288" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$)$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1072 #uuid "82b94457-e14d-5a58-a349-7d380e86804e" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235371 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nEarlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x-6 & >0 \\\\ -6x & >6 \\\\ x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235219 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235372 1069 "q-128289" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235372 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for the function $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The point of inflection is at $x\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235372 1072 #uuid "b65f104a-1a35-5e02-b86d-5c97ab5d1ec9" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235372 1074 "Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the points of inflection, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-6x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ To do that, we take two points, one to the left of $x=-1$ and another to the right of $x=-1.$ \n\nFor example, let's take the points $x=-2$ and $x=0.$ We compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the selected points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =-6(-2)-6=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-6(0)-6=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second derivative changes sign at $x=-1.$ \n\nSince the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235023 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235373 1069 "q-128332" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235373 1071 "The slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235373 1072 #uuid "148255e2-2a82-52fa-9fe8-d3d82db03fcc" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235373 1074 "The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(2).$ In part 1, we found\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{7(5-4\\cdot 2)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot (-3)}{(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-\\frac{21}{25}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235173 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235374 1069 "q-128333" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235374 1071 "At which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235374 1072 #uuid "4185e89f-8e3e-56fd-a474-e224ffa4b911" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235374 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 1, we obtained\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7(5-4x)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 7(5-4x) & =0 \\\\ 5-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\n* Second, we find the points in the domain of $f(x)$ where ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. Factoring our expression for ${f}^{′}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{7(5-4x)}{[(2x+1)(x-3)]^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3.$ But these points do not lie in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},3).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the only critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235197 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235375 1069 "q-128335" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235375 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235375 1072 #uuid "8c0064da-4356-5b95-8b94-94fe6fbae30f" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235375 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x)=7$ and $v(x)=2{x}^{2}-5x-3,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=0,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4x-5.\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x){v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot (2{x}^{2}-5x-3)-7\\cdot (4x-5)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\cdot (4x-5)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(5-4x)}{{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235061 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235107 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1069 "q-128336" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1071 "Determine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1072 #uuid "d386a0af-f386-5538-8165-48ec30e80948" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235376 1074 "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{7(4x-5)}{(2{x}^{2}-5x-3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the critical point $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 0-5)}{(2\\cdot {0}^{2}-5\\cdot 0-3)^{2}}=\\frac{35}{9}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{7(4\\cdot 2-5)}{(2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3)^{2}}=-\\frac{21}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4})$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $(\\frac{5}{4},3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n* At $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{4}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has $1$ local maximum and $0$ local minima on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235021 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235036 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1069 "q-128339" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1072 #uuid "333f44c6-e7aa-5f32-a7e4-9a58be270016" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235377 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235280 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235378 1069 "q-128340" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235378 1071 "${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235378 1072 #uuid "687b0f0d-b7fc-5c94-b602-200dc9ae2630" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235378 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235164 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235379 1069 "q-128660" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235379 1071 "Consider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235379 1072 #uuid "f8c9449b-c7f6-57bf-a86b-68a3efa54686" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235379 1074 "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{21}{25}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{7}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3}=-\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5-25f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+3.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+3)-(5-25f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot (g(2)+3)-(5-25f(2))\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2)+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\cdot (-\\frac{21}{25})\\cdot (-2+3)-(5-25\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-2+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =21-20 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235020 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235208 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1069 "q-131476" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1072 #uuid "f97091e5-4c8a-5c29-84a6-a20e717682cf" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235380 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235035 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235381 1069 "q-131477" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235381 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235381 1072 #uuid "a23363d4-1539-5b65-9754-68c957b99234" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235381 1074 "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235113 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235382 1069 "q-131478" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235382 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235382 1072 #uuid "1bd1cc7e-1467-5fa9-9364-b08bd7aa89f0" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235382 1074 "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $-1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $-1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235115 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235138 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1069 "q-131479" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1072 #uuid "47e117ef-6738-5bd8-aeaa-a7a0343ceecf" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235383 1074 "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function decrease without bound to $-\\infty .$   Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235213 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235384 1069 "q-131480" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235384 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=\\frac{4}{3},$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235384 1072 #uuid "a6ba6327-0941-5856-a970-a0df483bddf5" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235384 1074 "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot 4 & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235267 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235385 1069 "q-131481" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235385 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3})=$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235385 1072 #uuid "1547780d-30a4-520c-aff8-9cc9f3435542" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235385 1074 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3})=7{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)+8{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)-2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3}) & =7{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)+8{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)-2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+8\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =28-8-16 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235154 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235386 1069 "q-131482" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235386 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235386 1072 #uuid "c961b194-602b-5753-8bf9-ef5e3612c107" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235386 1074 "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in a neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235040 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235287 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1069 "q-131483" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1071 "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$and $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1072 #uuid "30c51f0c-7f0a-55e0-9cee-18ad253ce706" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235387 1074 "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235147 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235388 1069 "q-132974" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235388 1071 "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235388 1072 #uuid "5f760aed-6b31-5953-b3f9-00dcac8b3038" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235388 1074 "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235057 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235389 1069 "q-132975" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235389 1071 "${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235389 1072 #uuid "295b1f08-4feb-5011-b27e-88e7956a72df" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235389 1074 "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=2t$ and the denominator $h(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=2,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(2t)\\cdot (8t(2{t}^{2}+3))}{{((2{t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-16{t}^{2}(2{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}+6-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235246 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235390 1069 "q-132976" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235390 1071 "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235390 1072 #uuid "24759de2-6f72-5171-8480-c17060b3ce38" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235390 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $-\\frac{6}{125}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235281 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235391 1069 "q-132977" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235391 1071 "It can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235391 1072 #uuid "3ba61357-83e7-534d-8757-d228a1221f86" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235391 1074 "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 6-12{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6 & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235105 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235392 1069 "q-132978" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235392 1071 "The particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235392 1072 #uuid "632a77f1-9681-5dcd-aa11-3a8387a03565" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235392 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235251 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235393 1069 "q-132979" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235393 1071 "The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is equal to the rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate at this time. Calculate this velocity.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The velocity of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second equals\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235393 1072 #uuid "9106fff9-5741-53cb-af97-18e37fb205d3" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235393 1074 "The rate of change of the particle's $y$-coordinate when $t=1$ is given by ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn step 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12}{2+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235212 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235394 1069 "q-132980" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235394 1071 "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235394 1072 #uuid "76551198-3b30-5f90-a773-8f46f7e7ea21" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235394 1074 "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235030 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235162 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235167 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1069 "q-132981" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t},\n$$\n\nfind $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,C=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1072 #uuid "0042828e-27b2-590d-a108-55b85ccb3e29" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235395 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)}=\\frac{At+B}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{t}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p(t)=2{t}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $At+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $t(2{t}^{2}+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n6-12{t}^{2}=(At+B)t+C(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(0)^{2} & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 6 & =3C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(1)^{2} & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(2)(2(1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A+B+10 \\\\ A+B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $t=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-12(-1)^{2} & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(2)(2(-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ -6 & =A-B+10 \\\\ A-B & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-16, \\\\ A-B=-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution of this system is $A=-16$ and $B=0.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} & =\\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235175 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235396 1069 "q-132982" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235396 1071 "$\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,K.$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235396 1072 #uuid "7449abb3-586b-5fd3-93e0-df3f3d5afa01" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235396 1074 "Let $u=2{t}^{2}+3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=4t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can solve the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-16t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\cdot t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+K \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235218 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235397 1069 "q-132983" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235397 1071 "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235397 1072 #uuid "3c85a07f-1862-53e2-9986-87c4ee966897" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235397 1074 "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235106 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235150 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235240 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235273 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1069 "q-138783" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1071 "$$\nX+Y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,Z=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=[\\begin{aligned}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$ of a linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${t}_{11}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,{t}_{12}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${t}_{21}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,{t}_{22}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-4}}" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1072 #uuid "27a916f2-ae5e-57d0-a97a-ec9cecb80707" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235398 1074 "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nXZ+YZ=(X+Y)Z.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =XZ+YZ \\\\  & =(X+Y)Z \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{a