 and $h.$\"" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235237 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235585 1069 "q-394142" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235585 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235585 1072 #uuid "182c30e9-e7b7-5c1e-a9e5-3e098aabd14e" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235585 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{3/7}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on each open interval, but the limit of ${g}^{′}(x)$ as $x\\to {0}^{+}$ is not finite because $\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}$ becomes unbounded. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}})$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2/3}),\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. Because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2/3})$ approaches $1$ while $\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}$ becomes unbounded near $x=0,$ the derivative gets infinitely large. Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\"" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235277 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235586 1069 "q-394144" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235586 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}, & x<0 \\\\ x+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)=4x-\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}},\\,h(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235586 1072 #uuid "7f5abb2a-067a-5b1d-97d8-e8dd5378e0bd" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235586 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On the open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-{x}^{2/3}, & x<0 \\\\ x+{x}^{2/5}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{1/3}}, & x<0 \\\\ 1+\\frac{2}{5{x}^{3/5}}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $g(x)=4x-\\sqrt[7]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\ng(x)=4x-{x}^{3/7},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=4-\\frac{3}{7{x}^{4/7}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)=3{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\nh(x)=3{x}^{2}+{x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=6x+\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the functions.\"" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235161 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235587 1069 "q-70148" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235587 1071 "![](images/d3/d3f77b108decdfe95fe06d1ace87ee7f844a4f937f8b67a9f14e750a09ae4d57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235587 1072 #uuid "a9d1dc6a-3cc4-5a49-870f-4f180b06cd63" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235587 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7f/7f29124776f69f370b3129313fd1c2fa850924f446c6f086dcacd170f1ca6d24.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2.5a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-b-2.5a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-b=-2.5a-b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2.5\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235003 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235588 1069 "q-77451" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235588 1071 "![](images/28/28faea3a553c505a8a5ab9bc7dd6e784dd23a47c336c70d31218fbe249d57cff.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235588 1072 #uuid "adcdd06d-1586-51df-9df4-c005428c239d" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235588 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/52/52421034c2919620a0a9aae844db0eba2d396e5d6f1152d69bee40ff56c4d8ad.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235589 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235748 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235589 1062 #uuid "00bcac72-9a6b-5a44-a3ec-a5cf7b7af95f" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235589 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235373 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235590 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235675 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235590 1062 #uuid "0106719e-654b-54c8-b009-c0ab65f2c372" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235590 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235423 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235591 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235800 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235591 1062 #uuid "01afdcb8-687c-5175-8df6-cfe220d0b923" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235591 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235469 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235592 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235768 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235592 1062 #uuid "01dc8d52-2ae7-5911-afc7-c49662b4b596" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235592 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235390 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235593 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235776 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235593 1062 #uuid "028d050a-24ff-58c1-8d4a-d010753b2209" 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235593 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235368 13194139533384 17592186045585 true] [17592186235594 1062 #uuid "05c8d871-